[2026-09-25T01:39:55Z]
Redesigned with **different logic** as requested (agent, 2026-09-25 01:30 UTC).

The old chart was a cycle graph (fail → investigate → back to start, revert → rework → combine → back to start). Those back-edges are what made it render badly and made the "logic" hard to follow — GitHub's mermaid laid it out as a tangled blob.

New logic: a **straight 4-stage pipeline with one decision**, no loops and no back-edges — the gate rule is now literally the shape of the diagram:

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A["Idea or fix batch"] --> B["1. Small functional test"]
    B --> C["2. Medium validation run"]
    C --> D["3. Large benchmark vs baseline"]
    D --> E{"Better time and quality?"}
    E -->|yes| F["4. Keep and record"]
    E -->|no| G["Rework or revert"]
    F --> H["Next fix batch"]
    classDef step fill:#d9f7be,stroke:#389e0d;
    classDef stop fill:#ffccc7,stroke:#cf1322;
    class A,B,C,D,F,H step;
    class G stop;
```

What changed semantically:
- **Stages are numbered and strictly ordered** — you cannot reach "large benchmark" without passing small and medium (the issue #17 gate).
- **One verdict node** instead of two branch points; a failed verdict stops the batch (red) instead of looping back, so "keep only with evidence" is visible at a glance.
- The "next batch" arrow only comes out of **Keep**, i.e. surviving ideas are re-entered as a *new* batch rather than silently merged.

Same short labels (no `<br/>`, no emoji in edge labels), same keep/drop colours. Applied to `README.md` and `docs/09-optimization-strategy.md`; the full gate rule text stays in the bullets directly below the chart. Will render in the next push — if this layout still looks wrong to you, tell me the shape you'd prefer (e.g. left-to-right, or a simple numbered list instead of a chart) and I'll swap it.

